# Plain spoken prose

When the words are for a person to read — a chat answer, a comment reply, a Q&A, an article — write like a **chronicle** or an **objective documentary**. Who did what, in order. Ordinary spoken sentences. The reader makes up their own mind. Give them the facts they need to do that.

Same bar as [`docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md`](../../docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md). This rule also covers replies that never become a file.

## Do this

- Name events, dates, products, and what changed.
- One main fact per sentence.
- If the next sentence is a different person or a different claim, say how it sits against the one before it. **However** when they diverge. **And** or **the same line** when they agree. No extra word when it is just the next fact. Do not add a sentence that only explains the bridge.
- If a metaphor is the whole sentence, replace it with the fact.
- Read it aloud. If it would sound odd to a stranger, rewrite it.
- Keep documented charges. Plain is not softer. Plain is not poetry.

## Do not do this

- Poetry, trailer voice, manifesto cadence, stacked fragments (“Not as insult. Not as metaphor.”).
- Same-shape stacks that hide the relation (“Rezaei said Trump lost. Mojtaba said a party change would not end the hostility.”). The second sentence has to say whether it agrees.
- A neat closer that only sounds deep (“He is not the memory.” “The leftover fight is the soul.”).
- A one-sentence paragraph the draft wrote on its own. Join it to the paragraph it belongs to, or cut it. Keep it when Ari said that sentence, or told the draft to say it. A short beat he remembers saying stays, including two sentences (“A Democratic Congress was the bet. A majority under the filibuster was not the restoration the Democrats were waiting for.”).
- A metaphor doing the job of the fact (“the pain had to land now,” “the stories met on that price,” “the machine did not switch off,” “how a later vote gets to the van”). Say who did what.
- A sentence whose only job is the link or the wink (“NBC reported that line,” “A reader of American front pages heard”). Put the link on the words that already state the fact. Do not tell the reader what is ironic. The reader decides.
- Coaching the verdict (“that is why you must,” “the only real divider,” “notice what this means”) unless Ari asked for that sentence in those words — and then still say it as speech, not verse.
- **You** addressed at the reader, including a fact wearing “you” (“You cannot find every missile”). Say who, or state the fact. A quote may keep the speaker’s **you**.

Bold claims stay bold. Plain is not softer. Do not swap his sentence for a cooler one, and do not dress the fact up so it sounds like a closer. “Cool record” means ordinary speech. It does not mean a punch line.

```
Bad: The leftover fight is only whether the cage is holy. Isolated complaints rain on a parade. The stacked pattern is the attack.

Good: In 2013 The Weather Channel replaced the Local on the 8s jazz with production music. Viewers wrote. The new package stayed. A game company can do the same with a remake. One remake, taken alone, looks like a store page. The dates and the copies are what the reader has.
```

Code, commands, citations, and investigation tables stay as they are. This rule is for prose sentences.
